  "slug": "probabilistic-brain-connections-c47e",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test denoising, harmonization, fusion and decomposition methods on large, incomplete or reconstructed neuroimaging datasets.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is methodological: these preprints aim to make diffusion and functional MRI connectivity estimates more reliable under noise, reconstruction, missing modalities or pooling across cohorts. One study decomposes precisely aligned HCP probabilistic tractography connectomes with non-negative matrix factorization into white matter tracts and gray matter territories. Another reports that Marcenko-Pastur denoising of diffusion MRI weakens under common image reconstruction, and that a noise covariance estimated from reconstructed images restores it. StaGIC jointly infers regional circuit heterogeneity and structural connectivity from resting-state functional data. ProgMF fuses incomplete multimodal neuroimaging data and recovers sources linked to Alzheimer's diagnosis, cognition and amyloid PET. A deep learning harmonization study preserves tinnitus-related connectivity patterns when resting-state fMRI from patients and controls is pooled. The common advance is preserving biologically meaningful connectivity structure through each processing step.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
